is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router7
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the WAN transit link for OSPF between Campus 1 and Campus 2, specifically router7 eth3 (10.0.0.1/24) toward router10 eth2 (10.0.0.2/24).

Mechanism: The physical/down state of router7’s eth3 caused the OSPF adjacency on 10.0.0.0/24 to drop. OSPF re-ran SPF and installed an alternate, longer path to reach Campus 2 networks via 172.25.1.2 (router8) → 172.29.10.0/24 → router17 → 192.168.144.0/24 → router10. This extra hop count explains the reported higher latency without a full outage.

Localization: 
- On router7: eth3 is DOWN (ip -br link shows eth3 DOWN with 10.0.0.1/24), and “show ip ospf neighbor” does not list any neighbor on eth3. A probe “ping -I eth3 10.0.0.2” fails. The OSPF RIB shows 192.168.144.0/24 and even 10.0.0.0/24 now reachable via 172.25.1.2 (eth5), confirming reroute through router8.
- On router10: eth2 is UP with 10.0.0.2/24, but OSPF has no neighbor on 10.0.0.0/24; it only peers with router17 on 192.168.144.0/24.

Root cause: The physical/link failure on router7’s eth3 (carrier down) broke the direct OSPF adjacency to router10 on 10.0.0.0/24.

Corrective fix: Restore the WAN link between router7 eth3 and router10 eth2. On router7, verify the interface is not administratively down and bring it up (ip link set eth3 up), check/replace cabling or virtual link mapping, and confirm OSPF network statements (already present). Once eth3 is up/up, the OSPF neighbor with router10 should reestablish and traffic will revert to the lower-latency direct path. === END ===